.TH CHTTP_DEFAULT_CLIENT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_default_client \- give the default HTTP client of the process
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

chttpcli chttp_default_client(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function gives the default client of the process. The library
initializes that client only when it first needs it. The function is
thread-safe, and the library makes only one such client for each
process.

The default client uses the default settings. pool_size is the CPU
count, there are no timeouts, and TLS verification is on. You can
configure it with the same chttpclient_set_* functions as any other
client.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The handle of the default client, or CHTTPCLI_INVALID if the
initialization fails.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Do not give this handle to chttpclient_destroy. This module owns the
default client and destroys it automatically at the exit of the
process, from a handler that it registers with atexit(3) the first
time that it builds the default client. A call of chttp_default_client
from an exit handler that runs after that one gives CHTTPCLI_INVALID. A destroy by you does not crash that one call, because the
module knows the handle and clears its own reference to it. But the
library never builds the default client again. Every later call to
chttp_default_client, chttp_do, chttp_get and the other wrappers in
the process then fails cleanly and runs no request. This holds for the
rest of the life of that process. Do you need a client whose life you
control? Then make your own client with ccol_create_chttpclient or
with ccol_create_chttpclient_mp.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_create_chttpclient (3),
.BR chttpclient_destroy (3),
.BR chttp_do (3),
.BR chttp_get (3)
